WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
A binary format is a contract.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:12.437
The producer agrees to write bytes in a certain order, with certain meanings, and the consumer agrees to interpret those bytes only when the contract is satisfied.

3
00:00:12.437 --> 00:00:22.296
This applies whether the format is a public standard, an internal file layout, a network message, a memory snapshot, or a small structure stored on disk.

4
00:00:22.296 --> 00:00:37.761
That contract may include a magic value that identifies the format, a header that describes the rest of the data, a version field, flags, lengths, offsets, record counts, checksums, ordering rules, and limits on what combinations are valid.

5
00:00:37.761 --> 00:00:39.823
These are not decorative fields.

6
00:00:39.823 --> 00:00:48.136
They are the parser's first opportunity to decide whether it is looking at the expected thing and whether it can safely continue.

7
00:00:48.136 --> 00:00:51.100
Good validation happens before deeper parsing.

8
00:00:51.100 --> 00:01:03.021
The parser should identify the format, confirm the version is supported, reject or deliberately ignore unsupported flags according to policy, and check that required fields are present.

9
00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:05.728
Reserved fields deserve special attention.

10
00:01:05.728 --> 00:01:12.816
Sometimes the safest choice is to require them to be zero so future meanings do not get misread by older code.

11
00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:19.196
In other cases, compatibility requires ignoring them, but that should be an explicit design choice.

12
00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:25.382
Format documentation matters because it keeps the parser from becoming a collection of folklore.

13
00:01:25.382 --> 00:01:38.914
The documentation should define required fields, optional fields, valid ranges, byte order, alignment expectations, size limits, how records are ordered, what counts mean, and what failure behavior is expected.

14
00:01:38.914 --> 00:01:47.549
When the documentation is thin, developers tend to infer rules from examples, and examples rarely cover malformed or borderline input.

15
00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:51.351
The code should make its format assumptions easy to review.

16
00:01:51.351 --> 00:02:01.468
Named constants, small validation helpers, clear error paths, and staged parsing make it easier for another engineer to trace why a value is considered safe.

17
00:02:01.468 --> 00:02:07.525
A binary parser is not only a decoder; it is the executable expression of the format contract.

18
00:02:07.525 --> 00:02:14.549
If that contract is hidden in casts, pointer arithmetic, and unchecked assumptions, review becomes guesswork.
